{"seconds": 54.4, "exit": 0, "final": "Perfect! So after Rc8+ Bf8 Rcxf8+ Rxf8 Rxf8+, White is completely winning with Kxf8 or Kg7. And if Rc8+ Rxc8 Rf8+, then either Rxf8 and the rook is gone (White has won material), or Kxf8 e7+ and promotion is unstoppable.\n\nThis is clearly Rc8+, winning in all lines.\n\n**Best move:** Rc8+\n\n**Main line:** Rc8+ Rxc8 (if Bf8, then Rcxf8+ Rxf8 Rxf8+ wins; if Kxf8 is played on move 2, then after Rf8+ comes the promotion threat) Rf8+ Rxf8 (if Kxf8, then e7+ and promotion; if Bxf8, then Rxf8 wins) Rxc8 and White has won a full rook with a dominant position.\n\n**Motif:** Fork (the rook on c8 gives check and attacks the rook on a8 simultaneously)\n\n**Explanation:** After Black's queen move to e5, White's rooks dominate the 7th and 8th ranks. Rc8+ is a classic \"back-rank fork\"\u2014it gives check while attacking the undefended rook on a8. Black must recapture with the rook (or block with the bishop), but either way White wins decisive material. If Rxc8, then Rf8+ forces either the king to move (allowing promotion on e7) or Rxf8, which loses to Rxc8, winning the rook. This pattern\u2014using checks to win undefended pieces\u2014is essential at every level.\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Rc8+\", \"line\": [\"Rc8+\", \"Rxc8\", \"Rf8+\", \"Rxf8\", \"Rxc8\", \"Qxe6\"], \"motifs\": [\"fork\"]}\n```", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "r5k1/2R2Rbp/4P1p1/1B1pq3/P7/1P5P/6P1/7K w - - 6 41"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "r5k1/2R2Rbp/4P1p1/1B1pq3/P7/1P5P/6P1/7K w - - 6 41"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "r5k1/2R2Rbp/4P1p1/1B1pq3/P7/1P5P/6P1/7K w - - 6 41"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-line_view", "args": {"fen": "r5k1/2R2Rbp/4P1p1/1B1pq3/P7/1P5P/6P1/7K w - - 6 41", "square": "f7"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-line_view", "args": {"fen": "r5k1/2R2Rbp/4P1p1/1B1pq3/P7/1P5P/6P1/7K w - - 6 41", "square": "c7"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-square_info"